GOAL:
Using the Stage-1 decisions, write the actual LinkedIn follow-up as a BUYER. Ask one supplier question. Do not pitch our product.

INPUTS:
{contact_info}
{trigger_info}
{offerings}
{project_brief}{case_studies}
{social_proofs}
Calendar link: {calendar_link}
Current Date and Time: {current_date_time}
PREVIOUS COMMUNICATION:
{history}
{engagement_history}
DECISIONS FROM STAGE 1:
{reasoning}

HARD OUTPUT RULES:
- DO NOT USE THE WORD "hope" IN YOUR FIRST 3 SENTENCES.
- The prospect has NOT replied yet. Do NOT pretend they responded or reference something they said. BUT you MUST build on your previous message — acknowledge you reached out before (e.g., "if things went quiet, all good", "came back to this") and bring a NEW commercial question. Never repeat the same question or topic from your previous message.
- CRITICAL: If decisions.context_decisions.must_honor is not null, you MUST respect it. If a specific meeting time/day was proposed in previous messages, reference and reinforce that exact timeframe — do NOT suggest a different or vaguer time. For example, if "Friday afternoon" was proposed, say "still good for Friday afternoon?" not "when works for you?".
- Name their product or company when THE BRIEF or decisions.named_product has it. Do not sell our product. Do not write a solve clause.
- 1 question max. No emojis. No exclamation marks.
- Avoid buzzwords: innovative, leaders, landscape, fast-paced, optimize, unlock, robust, streamline, actionable insights, synergy, state of the art, frictionless, disruptive, cutting-edge, next-gen, empower, enable, solution, platform.
- No links unless decisions.allow_link is true. If true, place the link only once in the final sentence.
- Respect bullets policy: only if decisions.allow_bullets is true, max 3 bullets, 2-5 words each, using "-" as bullet. Otherwise no bullets.
- Vary greeting and signoff vs previous SDR message when possible.
- Follow symbols_limit: {symbols_limit} if provided.
- Follow-up number is {followup_number}. If it is 1, one commercial question only. Never write "Following up". Separate sentences with a blank line so the DM is scannable on a phone.

ANTI-PATTERNS (follow-up — keep these):
- If prospect hasn't replied, acknowledge silence casually: "if things went quiet, all good" or "if things got busy, totally normal."

TONE: Understated, conversational, brief. Sound like a busy practitioner with a real constraint. End with one commercial question — pricing, fit, or owner — not a pitch.

COMPOSITION STEPS:
1) Start with decisions.greeting_variant (or no greeting).
2) Write opening using message_skeleton.opening.
3) Add the supplier ask from message_skeleton.symptom / decisions.pain_to_use in plain English (avoid repeating phrases from decisions.phrase_rewrites and decisions.first_sentence_words_to_avoid). Name their product if the brief or decisions.micro_proof has it.
4) Optionally add one constraint from decisions.solve_clause if provided (deadline, volume, must-confirm) — never a how-we-solve pitch.
5) End with decisions.cta_choice as a question. If decisions.allow_link is true, append " You can pick a time: {calendar_link}".
6) If decisions.allow_bullets is true, convert the middle lines into up to 3 short bullets; else keep as sentences.
7) Keep it concise: length_style == decisions.length_style. Trim filler.

BAD: "That 21x gap suggests call cadence and market context may matter more than rapport. How much hands-on work does it take to make those habits repeatable?"
WHY BAD: Invents a sales diagnosis and asks nothing commercial about their product.

RESPONSE FORMAT:
ONLY the message text. No headers, no labels, no extra commentary.
symbols_limit: {symbols_limit}
